  "query_preview": "/Monday through Friday/...\nIssued 320 AM MST FRI NOV 13 2026\nCome Monday, the second Pacific upper trough digging into the \nnorthwest region will bring another cold front across the area. \nThe system will bring a 50-70% chance of light precipitation \nMonday through Tuesday. Snow levels will drop down to 4000-6000 \nft MSL by Tuesday, lowest across eastern Oregon. A brief dry \nperiod is expected early Wednesday morning before the next \nsystem moves in. This deep upper-level low will move overhead \nfrom mid to late week, bringing colder air than previous \nsystems. Uncertainty remains over this system's exact pattern as\nit either digs across our area as an upper trough or dive down \ninto the California coast as a closed upper low. For now, \nprecipitation chances remain at 40-70% late Wednesday through \nThursday, with temperatures favored to cool a few more degrees \nand snow levels around 3000-4500 feet. Come early Friday, our \narea is projected to be under a strong northerly flow as the \nupper low digs into the Desert Southwest region. Light remnant \nmountain snow showers are possible Friday, as snow levels are \nprojected to drop down to valley floor levels by Friday.",
